# Line-ending and diff behaviour.
#
# Without this file, git guesses per-file. That guessing is mostly harmless
# for source code and actively dangerous for the two binary-ish things this
# repo now contains — see below.

# Normalise text files to LF in the repository. Windows checkouts still get
# CRLF locally; this only governs what's stored. It also stops the
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings on every `git add`.
* text=auto

# Explicitly text, so nothing gets misdetected as binary.
*.py    text diff=python
*.md    text
*.ts    text
*.tsx   text
*.json  text
*.yaml  text
*.yml   text
*.css   text
*.txt   text

# Shell scripts must keep LF even when checked out on Windows — a CRLF in a
# shebang line makes the script unrunnable on Linux, which is exactly where
# the deployed service runs.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# BINARY. This is the one that matters.
#
# sme_credit_model.pkl is a pickled XGBoost model, committed deliberately so
# the deployed /explain endpoint has something to load. If git ever applied
# line-ending conversion to it, every 0x0A byte inside the binary would be
# rewritten and the file would arrive on the server corrupted — loading it
# would fail with a confusing unpickling error that looks nothing like its
# actual cause.
*.pkl   binary

# CSVs are treated as binary too: they're data, not source. Diffing a 150,000
# row file is pointless, and conversion risks mangling any field that legally
# contains a newline inside quotes.
*.csv   binary
